    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d477">

                <pb facs="0579" n="1413" xml:id="pg1413"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/261a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-14T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 14, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6889. For Sheldon [from
                    War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 197. Referring our 195, March 12, in addition to other matters mentioned to
                    Dutch Minister, McCormick also conveyed to him that even in case United States
                    and Allies were obliged to requisition, Holland’s trade with her colonies would
                    be facilitated. This is very important to allay the fears of Holland as to her
                    colonial trade.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Stevens and
                        Garrett.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
